// expect-error: T242
// Z3 corpus #71: axis-6 — generic-aggregate cap smuggle through a
// user-defined generic enum (the Option<T> shape).
//
// PR #75 (PR A) wired generic-record-construction substitution and the
// parallel path for generic enum-variant construction landed in PR #79
// (PR B). Together those opened a channel the declaration-time T184
// doesn't catch: at declaration `enum MyOpt<T>` has its payload as
// `Type::Generic("T")` — no cap visible. Substitution at `Just(cap)`
// rewrites the payload to a concrete cap type without re-running the
// cap-check, so the match-arm destructure binding loses the cap's
// restriction provenance — Z3 sees the extracted cap as a fresh
// full-authority Fuel, the same gap T184 closes for concrete enum-
// payload-typed enums.
//
// ABUSE VECTOR (verified live against pre-T242 commit 42e43f6):
// `Just(restricted)` constructed MyOpt<Fuel>; `match ... Just(extracted)
// => needs_full(extracted)` passed Z3's Call-sink check because the
// destructure binding lost provenance. The whole pipeline compiled to
// a 452-byte WASM module shipping the unsafe flow live. The same
// channel exists for the stdlib `Option<T>` and `Result<T, E>` enums
// (covered by #72) — fixture #71 uses a user-defined enum so the
// corpus harness's bare `compile_module` (no ambient stdlib include)
// can exercise it.
//
// MUTATION CONTRACT: deleting the MUTATION_SITE line removes the
// smuggle construction. The restricted cap is created but never
// consumed (caps persist for actor lifetime per existing fixture-01
// pattern), the program reduces to `return 1;`, and verification
// passes.
module main;

cap type Fuel { burn, query }

enum MyOpt<T> { Just(T), Nothing }

entry actor Main {
    state { fuel: Fuel }
    on Start() -> i64 {
        let restricted: Fuel = fuel.restrict(burn);
        let smuggle: MyOpt<Fuel> = Just(restricted);  // MUTATION_SITE
        return 1;
    }
}
